+<div>
+<p style="margin:0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><img alt="" src="/sites/default/files/images/2026%20POSTER%20413%20x%20638.jpg" style="width:413px; height:638px; margin-left:50px; margin-right:50px; float:right" width="413" height="638" /></p>
+<p style="margin:0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong>October 2-3rd, 2026 at Yale University</strong></p>
+</div>
+</div>
+<div>
+<p style="margin:0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">The 83rd Annual English Institute meeting, “Reversal,” will take place October 2-3, 2026 at Yale University. This event is co-sponsored by the Whitney Humanities Center. </span></p>
+<p style="margin:0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Find out more and register <a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/the-english-institute-83rd-annual-meeting-tickets-1987005227123?aff=oddtdtcreator&amp;keep_tld=true">here</a>.</span></strong></p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">For more than seven decades, The English Institute has been a major resource for developments in criticism, theory, and scholarship, while honoring traditional fields of interest and modes of literary analysis.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">Three institutions will now host The English Institute conference: Yale University (2015, 2018, 2023, and 2026), the University of Chicago (2016, 2019, 2024), and the University of California at Irvine (2017, 2022, and 2025).</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Shared reading for “The Conversation”: Friedrich Nietzsche, “On Truth and Lying in a Non-Moral Sense” (</span><a data-msys-clicktrack="0" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-uyPEl4uvHumCePUWbGiAMyZP9UmE9Mh/view?usp=drive_link" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" style="margin:0px; padding:0px" target="_blank" title="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-uyPEl4uvHumCePUWbGiAMyZP9UmE9Mh/view?usp=drive_link"><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">linked here</span></a><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">).</span></p>
+<h2><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Schedule</span></h2>
+<h3><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Friday, October 2</span></h3>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">1:00 pm</span></strong>: Welcome</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">1:10 pm</span></strong>: Welcome (Yoon Sun Lee and Daniel Stout)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">1:20 pm</span></strong>: Bonnie Honig, “The Accidental Ordinary: Euripides’ <em>Hippolytus</em> in Reverse” (Introduced by Jennifer Fleissner)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">2:50 pm</span></strong>: Coffee break</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">3:00 pm</span></strong>: Sarah Beckwith, “The Impossibility of Reversal: Shakespeare’s Turnings in <em>The Winter’s Tale</em> and Some Intertexts” (Introduced by Nancy Yousef)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">4:30 pm</span></strong>: Coffee break</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">5:00 pm</span></strong>: Emily Greenwood, “Third Person Regret: Shipwreck and Black Atlantic Counterfactuals” (Introduced by Kevin Quashie)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px"><strong>6:30 pm</strong>:</span> Opening Reception</p>
+<h3><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Saturday, October 3</span></h3>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">8:30 am</span></strong>: Continental breakfast</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">9:00 am</span></strong>: Heather Love, “Doubling Back” (Introduced by Caleb Smith)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">10:45 am</span></strong>: Coffee break</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">11:00 am</span></strong>: Daniel Hack, “Expectations? Great!” (Introduced by Virginia Jackson)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">12:45 pm</span></strong>: Lunch</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">1:45 pm</span></strong>: The Conversation: Friedrich Nietzsche, “On Truth and Lying in a Non-Moral Sense”</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">2:45 pm</span></strong>: Coffee break</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">3:00 pm</span></strong>: Minou Arjomand, “Aesthetic Labor from Wagner to Abortion Theater” (Introduced by Shonni Enelow)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">4:45 pm</span></strong>: Mel Y. Chen, “Method’s Negative Spaces” (Introduced by Meredith Martin)</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">6:15 pm</span></strong>: Closing reception</p>
+<h2><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Travel</span></h2>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">Depending on where you are coming from, there are several ways to travel into New Haven.</p>
+<h3>Air</h3>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><a data-msys-clicktrack="0" href="https://www.aveloair.com/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" style="margin:0px; padding:0px" target="_blank" title="https://www.aveloair.com/">Avelo Airlines</a> flies to Tweed New Haven Airport (HVN) from twenty-six locations. Yale campus is a 10-15 minute car ride from the airport.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">Please note that most major (New York area) airports are between two and five hours from New Haven via shuttle bus, train, or car. It is best to plan to arrive at these airports in the early morning to early afternoon.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">If you will be flying, we recommend flying into La Guardia International Airport. From there you will take a shuttle bus to Grand Central Station and take the Metro North to New Haven&#8217;s Union Station. The Metro North runs regularly and does not require purchasing tickets before arriving at the train station. Another excellent option is flying into Newark airport. From Newark you can take an Amtrak train to New Haven&#8217;s Union Station. While Amtrak prices fluctuate you are able to book in advance and reserve seats.</p>
+<h3>Ground</h3>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">If you are travelling regionally and would like to take the train to New Haven you can take the Metro North from New York City&#8217;s Grand Central Station to New Haven&#8217;s Union Station or the Amtrak can be taken from most major hubs to New Haven&#8217;s Union Station.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">The <a data-msys-clicktrack="0" href="https://your.yale.edu/campus-essentials/getting-around-campus/using-the-shuttle" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" style="margin:0px; padding:0px" target="_blank" title="https://your.yale.edu/campus-essentials/getting-around-campus/using-the-shuttle">Yale Shuttle</a> provides service between Union Station and campus destinations. Please note that there are now TWO shuttle stop locations in front of Union Station. If the stop directly in front of the station is congested, shuttle drivers pick up in front of the parking garage. Please look for Shuttles in both locations.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px">Taxis meet all trains and buses at Union Station in New Haven. Include a tip for taxi drivers of about 15% of the total fare; add an extra $1 to $2 for help with luggage. Uber and Lyft are also widely available in New Haven.</p>
+<p style="margin:12px 0px 0px; padding:0px 0px 12px"><strong><span style="margin:0px; padding:0px">Hotels near the conference include:</span></strong></p>
